From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tomaz Zaman <tomaz@mono.si>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 15:30:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] wireguard: seal a forwarded packet in its receive buffer

A packet the NIC's classifier has already routed to a WireGuard device
still pays for an skb, the staging queue, the encryption workers, the
in-order transmit stage and the UDP and IP output paths, all to wrap it
in four headers and a cipher.

wg_fastpath_seal() does that work in the buffer the NIC received the
packet into: it finds the peer by the inner destination, takes a nonce
from the current keypair, pads and encrypts in place, and writes the
outer Ethernet, IPv4 and UDP header send4() would build, cached per
peer for a second. The caller transmits the result from the same
buffer. An IPv6 endpoint, an unresolved neighbour, an egress the caller
cannot transmit on, a packet over the MTU or a peer without a usable
keypair are declined before the buffer is written, and the packet
takes the ordinary path. Timers and the rekey test run a few times a
second rather than per packet.

The CPU that receives a peer's packets also carries every ACK of the
flows sealed for it; a flow sealed there starves that receive path and
with it every flow of the tunnel. The receive path records its CPU, and
a frame arriving on it is declined with -EXDEV for the caller to seal
on another CPU.
---
